# 1. Health and human-services case management

## A. Current state and hard numbers

### Professional standards and workforce

* **Latest CMSA standard.** The latest full *CMSA Standards of Practice for Case Management* remains the **2022 revision**. It reorganized 15 standards around execution of the case-management process. In October 2024 CMSA added **Standard Q: Diversity, Equity, Inclusion and Belonging and Health Equity**; it is an addendum, not a new full edition [H1].
* **Certification scale.** CCMC/The Commission reported **49,789 active CCM certificants** across the April, August and December 2025 cycles; its public wording is “more than 50,000” active CCMs and more than 71,000 certified since 1993. The 2025 exam-cycle total was **5,041 candidates with a 74% pass rate**. Because certification is optional and titles vary, this is not the total US case-management workforce [H2].
* **Aging pipeline.** CCMC’s 2024 Job Task Analysis obtained **2,145 usable responses**. Thirty-six percent held a master’s and 3% a doctorate; 41% said employers required certification; nearly 29% had more than 20 years in case management; only 4% were under 35; almost 15% were 65 or older, up from 7% in 2019. The survey was purposive and had roughly a 3.2% usable response rate, so it describes respondents, not a probability sample [H3].
* **Adherence guidance.** CMSA’s current *Case Management Adherence Guide* is **CMAG 2020**. It combines health-literacy, readiness, medication-knowledge, social-support and motivational tools. The instruments have validation histories, but the guide itself should not be mistaken for an RCT showing that the complete bundle improves hard outcomes [H4].

### Medicare care-management architecture

Medicare now pays for a layered set of longitudinal coordination services:

* **CCM:** CPT 99490/99439 for clinical-staff time, and 99487/99489 for complex CCM; eligibility generally requires at least two chronic conditions expected to last 12 months or until death. The base non-complex service starts at 20 minutes/month. CPT 99491 covers personally delivered physician/QHP time [H5].
* **PCM:** CPT 99424–99427 supports management of one serious, high-risk chronic condition, using 30-minute base increments depending on whether the physician/QHP or clinical staff performs the work.
* **TCM:** CPT 99495 requires contact within two business days, at least moderate-complexity decision-making and a face-to-face visit within 14 days; 99496 requires high complexity and a visit within seven days [H6].
* **CHI and PIN:** Since 2024, HCPCS G0019/G0022 (Community Health Integration) and G0023/G0024, with peer-support variants, pay trained auxiliary personnel—including community health workers—for navigation around non-medical barriers and serious illness. CMS’s 2026 rule replaced some “social determinants” wording with broader **“upstream drivers,”** expanded eligible initiating encounters and clarified participation by behavioral-health professionals [H7].
* **APCM:** Effective 1 January 2025, HCPCS **G0556, G0557 and G0558** pay a monthly bundle according to medical and social complexity. APCM absorbs elements of CCM, PCM, TCM, interprofessional consultation and communications technology, reducing minute-by-minute billing. It points toward prospective, relationship-based primary-care payment rather than a stack of narrowly timed tasks [H8].
* **2026 PFS:** The CY 2026 final rule created separate conversion factors for qualifying APM participants and others, retained/expanded advanced primary-care policy, added behavioral-health integration options around APCM and refined CHI/PIN language. These are payment rules, not evidence that every billed service improves outcomes [H9].

### What does the effectiveness evidence show?

The answer is **targeting- and model-dependent**, not a universal positive effect.

* A 2015 systematic review/meta-analysis of **36 studies** (28 RCTs) of primary-care case management for people at risk of hospitalization found no significant difference in total cost, mortality, primary-care use or secondary-care use; patient satisfaction improved slightly [H10]. A 2020 review of reviews similarly found strong evidence for better guideline adherence and satisfaction, but no survival effect and no consistent reduction in hospital use.
* For severe mental illness, the 2017 Cochrane review found intensive case management reduced hospital use versus standard care by **0.86 inpatient days per month** (95% CI −1.37 to −0.34; 3,595 participants, 24 RCTs; low-certainty evidence), improved retention and some social outcomes, but differed little from other non-intensive case-management programs [H11].
* For homelessness, Ponka et al. screened 13,811 citations and included **56 primary studies**. Standard case management had limited, short-lived effects; intensive case management, assertive community treatment and critical-time intervention produced better housing and selected health outcomes. Housing itself, income and service availability confound any attempt to attribute results to “case management” alone [H12].
* For community-dwelling older adults, Mieda et al.’s 2026 review included **15 trials and 7,995 participants**. Very-low-certainty pooled evidence showed little or no difference in institutionalization, mortality or admission; physical quality of life improved only slightly (**SMD 0.08, 95% CI 0.00–0.15**, moderate certainty) [H13].
* Transitional-care evidence is stronger when the intervention is intensive and protocolized. In Naylor’s older-adult trial, intervention patients had **104 versus 162 readmissions** and mean total costs of **$7,636 versus $12,481**. Replication and implementation studies support benefit, but “TCM billing” is not automatically equivalent to the nurse-led Transitional Care Model [H14].
* Hospital-at-home evidence is promising but mixed. A Cochrane admission-avoidance review found little or no six-month mortality difference (RR 0.88, 95% CI 0.68–1.13; five trials, 1,502 participants) or clear readmission reduction. CMS’s observational waiver study found generally lower 30-day mortality than matched facility care, but selection remains a major threat to causal inference [H15].

### Scale of value-based and home-based coordination

* ACO REACH had **103 ACOs, about 2.5 million aligned beneficiaries and 160,837 providers** in PY2025. CMS’s independent evaluation estimated that PY2024 reduced gross spending **2.2% ($309 per beneficiary-year; $706.1 million)**, yet after incentives and spillovers net spending rose a statistically uncertain **0.2% ($55.3 million)**. Separate benchmark reconciliation reported $2.511 billion gross savings and $988.3 million retained by CMS. Those figures answer different questions and should not be blended [H16].
* Medicare Advantage enrolled roughly **34 million people—about 54% of eligible Medicare beneficiaries—in 2025**. MA can finance navigation and supplemental benefits more flexibly than fee-for-service Medicare, but prior authorization, favorable selection, coding intensity and weak public reporting complicate claims that coordination alone causes savings.
* As of 24 July 2026, CMS listed **364 approved hospital CCNs across 136 systems in 37 states** for Acute Hospital Care at Home. A February 2026 federal package extended the waiver through **30 September 2030**, changing the outlook from repeated short extensions to a medium-term implementation runway [H17].

## B. Key trends, 2024–2026

1. **From discrete codes to longitudinal bundles.** APCM is the clearest signal: pay for the relationship, access, planning and coordination package rather than every minute separately.
2. **Social care becomes billable infrastructure.** CHI/PIN and Medicaid state-plan pathways legitimize CHWs and navigators, but reimbursement varies and community organizations still carry uncompensated referral work.
3. **Risk-bearing organizations internalize case management.** ACO REACH, MA and Medicaid managed care make avoidable utilization financially salient. The danger is that “high risk” becomes synonymous with “high cost,” reproducing the Obermeyer bias discussed in §2.
4. **Home becomes a care setting.** Hospital-at-home, remote monitoring and post-acute transition programs turn the case manager into a logistics and safety orchestrator across home, pharmacy, transport, caregivers and escalation pathways.
5. **Person-centred planning gains regulatory weight.** Medicaid HCBS rules require goals, preferences, informed choice and conflict-of-interest safeguards—not simply an administratively complete care plan [H18].

## C. Trajectory to 2030–2035

* **Most likely:** tiered models—automated low-intensity outreach; CHW/navigation for social and access barriers; licensed case managers for multimorbidity, transitions and contested decisions; specialist teams for the highest-risk cases.
* **Payment:** APCM-like bundles and value-based contracts will expand faster than pure fee-for-service case-management codes. ACO REACH ends after 2026, but its accountable-care logic and successor models will continue.
* **Work location:** the home and community will absorb more acute/post-acute activity through 2030, backed by the AHCAH extension.
* **Measurement:** purchasers will demand patient-goal attainment, days at home, burden, equity and total cost—not just contacts completed and admissions avoided.
* **Named forecasts:** HRSA’s 2023–2038 projections anticipate a national RN shortfall of 108,960 FTEs and an 11% nonmetropolitan shortfall by 2038 (§6); BLS projects rapid CHW growth. These workforce forecasts make delegation plus digital augmentation structurally likely.

## D. Controversies and evidence gaps

* **Intervention heterogeneity:** “case management” ranges from a few telephone calls to 24/7 multidisciplinary ACT. Pooled nulls may conceal effective submodels; positive vendor studies may select the highest-opportunity patients.
* **Regression to the mean and avoidable-use claims:** high-cost patients often become cheaper without intervention. Evaluations need concurrent controls and transparent attribution.
* **Equity versus savings:** social-needs screening without available resources can document deprivation without resolving it. Cost-based risk scores may under-identify underserved groups.
* **Workforce substitution:** CHWs extend cultural and community reach but should not become low-cost substitutes for licensed judgment or unpaid emotional labor.
* **Home safety and caregiver burden:** hospital-at-home studies under-measure housing suitability, caregiver work, digital exclusion and failed escalation.
* **Certification denominator:** active CCMs are measurable; the total multidisciplinary case-management workforce is not. Occupational codes fragment nurses, social workers, navigators and utilization staff.

