# 2. AI and agentic AI in healthcare case management

## A. Current state and hard numbers

### Risk stratification and care-management platforms

Epic, Optum, Innovaccer, Arcadia and Lightbeam sell overlapping population-health capabilities: patient identity resolution, risk scoring, care gaps, registries, attribution, work queues and care-plan documentation. Their public evidence is uneven.

* **Epic** publishes HTI-1 “predictive DSI” source attributes and model-risk practices, but performance depends on the local population, outcome definition and implementation. EHR-integrated models should be revalidated locally, not treated as transportable medical facts [AI1].
* **Optum** reports that a customer reduced heart-failure readmissions about 27% in one year using Optum Analytics. This is a vendor case study without randomized attribution [AI2].
* **Innovaccer** claimed in 2025 that new care-management copilots reduced documentation time 28% and increased engagement 20%; **Lightbeam** and **Arcadia** publish similarly favorable savings and admission claims. These are useful implementation signals but generally lack independent controls, preregistered endpoints and full denominators. Product selection should separate platform usability from causal clinical efficacy.

The decisive historical warning remains Obermeyer et al. The commercial algorithm they studied used **future cost as a proxy for health need**. At the same score Black patients were sicker; correcting the proxy would have increased Black patients selected for extra help from **17.7% to 46.5%** [AI3]. “Race-blind” inputs therefore did not produce race-neutral access.

### Ambient documentation and case-work copilots

Ambient AI moved from pilot to infrastructure in 2024–2026. Vanderbilt made an EHR-integrated ambient scribe available to more than **2,400 ambulatory clinicians** in January 2025 [AI4]. A 2026 national study reported that nearly two-thirds of US hospitals using Epic had adopted an ambient product, with substantial organizational disparities. Pre/post and early randomized studies generally find less documentation time and cognitive load, but evidence on note accuracy, downstream coding, patient disclosure and long-term burnout is thinner.

For case managers, the analogous uses are call summaries, problem-list extraction, draft care plans, handoff notes, referral letters and appeal packets. These are lower-risk when the source record is retained and the user must verify the draft. They become high-risk when generated text silently becomes the factual record or triggers eligibility/coverage action.

### Prior authorization and utilization management

LLM and multi-agent vendors now automate chart retrieval, criteria matching, form completion, status checks and appeal drafting. A 2026 systematic review found only **16 eligible studies**: 62.5% addressed payer review/decision-making, 18.8% post-denial appeals, 12.5% initial submission and 6.3% early initiation; patient participation was rarely studied [AI5]. Accuracy claims of 75–95% from Plenful, RISA Labs and similar firms are usually vendor-defined task metrics, not independent measures of wrongful denial or patient harm.

CMS-0057-F creates a more important near-term baseline than any one model [AI6]:

* Beginning primarily **1 January 2026**, covered payers must decide urgent medical-item/service requests within **72 hours** and standard requests within **seven calendar days**, give a specific denial reason and publicly report prior-authorization metrics. Federally facilitated exchange QHPs are excluded from some timing provisions.
* Beginning primarily **1 January 2027**, impacted payers must implement FHIR-based Prior Authorization, Provider Access, Payer-to-Payer and expanded Patient Access APIs. Providers may request data and send authorization information electronically.
* The rule applies to MA, Medicaid and CHIP FFS/managed care, and specified exchange plans; it does not initially create an identical regime for every commercial plan or drug authorization.

The implementation stack is HL7 FHIR R4 plus Da Vinci implementation guides—Coverage Requirements Discovery (CRD), Documentation Templates and Rules (DTR), and Prior Authorization Support (PAS). PAS 2.1.0 remained a published **trial-use, maturity-level-3** guide in late 2024; a 2.2.1 continuous build existed in August 2026. “Standards-based” therefore still involves versioning and operational testing [AI7].

### Interoperability and algorithm transparency

* **TEFCA:** national exchange went live with five QHINs in December 2023. By August 2025, ASTP reported **10 QHINs and more than 9,200 participating organizations**; Oracle became the eleventh QHIN in November 2025 [AI8]. TEFCA improves discovery and exchange but does not itself normalize social-service data, fix consent segmentation or guarantee closed-loop referrals.
* **HTI-1:** effective February 2024, with the revised Decision Support Intervention criterion replacing the old CDS criterion in the Base EHR definition on **1 January 2025**. Certified modules must expose source attributes for predictive DSIs—developer, purpose, intended population, inputs, validation, performance, fairness and risk-management information—and support feedback [AI9]. It is transparency, not FDA-style premarket approval.
* **FDA:** the January 2025 lifecycle guidance for AI-enabled device software remained **draft**. FDA emphasizes total-product-lifecycle risk management and predetermined change-control plans. Many administrative care-management and payer algorithms are not medical devices, leaving a regulatory gap [AI10].
* **EU AI Act:** healthcare AI may be high-risk when it is a safety component of a regulated product or falls in an Annex III rights-sensitive use. As amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, most obligations for stand-alone Annex III high-risk systems move to **2 December 2027**, and product-embedded Annex I systems to **2 August 2028**. Required controls include risk management, data governance, technical documentation, logging, transparency to deployers, human oversight, accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity [AI11]. This supersedes older summaries that still cite August 2026/2027.

### Litigation and state regulation

* **UnitedHealth/naviHealth nH Predict:** *Estate of Lokken v. UnitedHealth Group* alleges that the model drove premature termination of post-acute coverage and that many appealed denials were reversed. In February 2025 the court allowed core claims to proceed while dismissing others; in March 2026 a magistrate ordered broad discovery. As of 27 September 2026, no merits judgment had established the alleged “90% error rate”; it remains a complaint allegation [AI12].
* **Cigna PxDx:** the complaint alleged more than 300,000 claims were denied in two months after very brief physician review. On 31 March 2025, the Eastern District of California granted in part and denied in part Cigna’s motion to dismiss; standing and whether particular plaintiffs were actually subjected to PxDx narrowed the case. Again, allegations are not adjudicated facts [AI13].
* **California SB 1120:** signed 28 September 2024 and effective 1 January 2025, it requires that AI/algorithmic tools in utilization management use clinical and other relevant information, apply consistently and without unlawful discrimination, and **not supplant a licensed professional’s decision** on medical necessity [AI14]. By 2026, KFF identified a widening set of state rules, including Texas legislation, but definitions and enforcement differ [AI15].

## B. Key trends, 2024–2026

1. **From prediction to action.** Platforms are moving from a risk score placed in a queue to an agent that assembles evidence, drafts a plan, contacts the member and opens tasks.
2. **From proprietary interfaces to API-mediated authorization.** CMS-0057-F and Da Vinci make authorization a computable workflow, although clinical criteria remain partly proprietary.
3. **Ambient capture becomes the case record’s front door.** Voice-to-structured-data can reduce clerical load but also scale subtle factual errors.
4. **Governance shifts from “model accuracy” to lifecycle accountability.** HTI-1, FDA lifecycle guidance and the EU AI Act require intended-use documentation, subgroup validation, monitoring, change control and human authority.
5. **Denial automation becomes a legal-reputational risk.** Payers increasingly distinguish decision support from final adverse action, preserve clinician sign-off and log rationales.

## C. Trajectory to 2030–2035

* By 2030, the common architecture will be a **FHIR-connected, retrieval-grounded case copilot** with constrained tools: retrieve records, check criteria, draft communications, schedule actions and escalate exceptions.
* Fully autonomous approval of simple, rules-satisfied requests may become common; fully autonomous adverse determinations will remain contested and jurisdiction-dependent.
* TEFCA plus payer APIs will reduce record chasing, but consent, identity, behavioral-health segmentation and social-service interoperability will remain bottlenecks.
* High-risk systems will need continuous subgroup and drift monitoring. “Human in the loop” will mature from a click-through sign-off into defined review time, competence, override authority and audit sampling.
* Gartner’s forecast that more than 40% of agentic projects will be cancelled by the end of 2027 (§3) is especially relevant: brittle integration, unclear value and inadequate controls—not raw model capability—will stop many health deployments.

## D. Controversies and evidence gaps

* **Proxy choice:** cost, utilization and documentation density encode unequal access.
* **Automation bias:** a nominal reviewer may rubber-stamp a recommendation, particularly under productivity quotas.
* **Opacity versus trade secrets:** patients need reasons and appealable evidence; vendors resist revealing models and criteria.
* **No shared denominator for errors:** vendors report extraction accuracy or minutes saved, while patients care about delayed treatment, wrongful denial and uncorrected records.
* **Regulatory perimeter:** administrative agents can materially affect health while falling outside FDA device authority.
* **Model drift and local validity:** EHR models and LLM workflows change with population, documentation, benefits and practice patterns.
* **Dual automation race:** providers automate submissions and appeals while payers automate review, potentially increasing volume without resolving low-value authorization requirements.

