# 4. Legal and judicial case management

## A. Current state and hard numbers

### Court workload and core systems

The National Center for State Courts’ Court Statistics Project reports about **70 million state-court filings in 2024**, up 4% from 2023 but still 27% below 2012; traffic matters were about 46% of incoming cases [L1]. National totals conceal major differences in case categories, data definitions, pending inventory and age. A court can reduce filings yet retain a serious backlog; clearance rates and time-to-disposition are more meaningful than filings alone.

Federal data show a mixed picture. In the year ending 30 September 2025, US Courts of Appeals received **41,824 filings, up 5.1%**, and terminated 40,411, up only 0.8%, so pending inventory rose [L2]. Immigration, multidistrict litigation, vacancies and local practice create court-specific bottlenecks.

Two established court-CMS suppliers illustrate the market:

* **Tyler Odyssey** integrates case records, calendaring, financials, public access, jury and e-filing. Tyler reported converting 33 million cases, 64 million parties and nearly $4 billion in payments during a 2014 migration wave; current jurisdiction counts are not independently standardized [L3].
* **Thomson Reuters C-Track** is a configurable web platform spanning trial and appellate courts, e-filing, public portals and court analytics [L4]. Product descriptions establish capability, not comparative performance.

E-filing is now basic infrastructure but not uniformly universal. Ohio’s Supreme Court, for example, received 908 of 1,716 new 2025 cases electronically and 7,666 total portal submissions. There is no clean, current national percentage covering every state, level and case type.

### ODR and digital access

Online dispute resolution combines electronic intake, asynchronous negotiation, mediation, document exchange and sometimes automated triage. NCSC promotes ODR as an access channel [L5], but causal evidence is thinner than adoption rhetoric. Greiner, Matthews and Owens’ field experiments show recruitment, take-up and implementation can break experimental designs; digital channels may reduce travel and default for some users while excluding people with limited connectivity, language access, disability accommodations or bargaining power [L6]. The next phase is therefore “online case resolution plus assisted digital service,” not online-only courts.

### AI guidance for judges and courts

* The **UK Judiciary** refreshed its guidance in April and again on **31 October 2025**. It warns judges not to enter private/confidential information, to verify outputs and citations, and to account for hallucination, bias and litigants’ use of AI [L7].
* The **US Administrative Office of the Courts** created an AI Task Force in early 2025 and distributed interim guidance in July 2025. As of September 2026 the federal judiciary was developing broader guidance; a Reuters report said the task force had made around 30 recommendations, including that judges not delegate judicial decision-making [L8].
* **NCSC’s September 2025 AI Readiness for the State Courts** provides governance, workforce, data, procurement and use-case assessment tools; its 2026 tracker maps state orders, rules and policies [L9].

Across jurisdictions, guidance converges on confidentiality, verification, disclosure where required, competence, non-delegation of judicial authority and preserving the record. Local filing orders remain inconsistent.

### Legal practice management and AI adoption

Clio, Litify, Filevine, MyCase/8am, Smokeball and enterprise systems organize matter intake, deadlines, documents, time, billing and client communications. Litify is built on Salesforce and is moving toward multi-step matter agents. Clio’s 2025 acquisitions of ShareDo and vLex expanded it from small/midsize practice management toward enterprise matter management and legal intelligence.

Adoption statistics vary because surveys ask different questions:

* ILTA’s 2025 survey covered **580 firms, 152,000 attorneys and 303,000 users**; 80% of responding firms were using or exploring generative AI, but firm-wide deployment was much rarer. Resistance to change (57%) and security/risk (54%) were leading barriers [L10].
* Thomson Reuters found **26% of professionals used GenAI at work in 2025**, almost double 14% in 2024 [L11].
* Clio reported 79% of legal professionals using AI in its 2025 data and **93% of surveyed midsize firms** using AI, up from 19% a year earlier. Those figures are vendor studies of different samples and should not be generalized to every law office [L12].
* An 8am 2026 survey reported 69% using general-purpose GenAI for work, while **54% of firms offered no AI training and 43% had no governance policy**. Again, sampling and wording drive the level.

Harvey says it serves more than 1,500 global customers; Thomson Reuters said CoCounsel exceeded one million professionals in 107 countries/territories by June 2026. These are vendor adoption counts, not independent active-use statistics.

Accuracy remains a constraint. Magesh et al. found leading legal RAG tools hallucinated or produced unsupported/incorrect information in roughly **17%–34%** of benchmark responses—better than general-purpose GPT-4 in the same test, but far from “hallucination-free” [L13]. The study tested a constructed query set and 2024 product versions; results should not be frozen in time, but they rebut categorical safety claims.

## B. Key trends, 2024–2026

1. **Matter systems and research systems converge.** Clio–vLex, CoCounsel/Westlaw and Litify agents bring authorities, matter facts and workflow into one surface.
2. **Court modernization shifts from digitizing documents to managing flow.** Dashboards, differentiated case management, text reminders, e-service and ODR target age and nonappearance, not merely paper elimination.
3. **Judicial AI policy becomes institutional.** Individual standing orders are giving way to judiciary-wide task forces, procurement standards and staff guidance.
4. **AI use becomes substantive.** Drafting, due diligence, chronology, discovery and contract review are moving beyond back-office experimentation.
5. **Audit and provenance become product requirements.** A legal answer must link to the authority and matter document it relies upon.

## C. Trajectory to 2030–2035

* By 2030, routine uncontested matters will be predominantly digital from filing through order, with human help available by design.
* Courts will use AI mainly for translation, summarization, transcript/search, scheduling and staff assistance; adjudicative recommendations will face much higher legitimacy barriers.
* Law firms will run “matter agents” inside permissioned workspaces, but lawyers will remain professionally responsible for filings, advice and client confidentiality.
* The market for practice management is forecast to grow from **$2.68 billion in 2025 to $4.66 billion in 2030 (11.6% CAGR)** by one publisher, while another estimates $2.97 billion in 2025 to $6.96 billion in 2031 (15.8%). The range reflects inconsistent scope (§8).
* By 2035, courts that fail to integrate assisted digital access may widen, rather than close, justice gaps.

## D. Controversies and evidence gaps

* **Backlog metrics are not standardized:** filings, pending cases, case age and weighted workload tell different stories.
* **Digital divide and procedural justice:** speed is not fairness; an ODR settlement may reflect convenience or coercion.
* **Confidentiality and privilege:** consumer GenAI, training retention and cross-matter retrieval can expose protected data.
* **Citation verification:** grounding lowers but does not eliminate hallucinations.
* **Unequal arms:** well-resourced repeat players can deploy sophisticated agents against self-represented litigants.
* **Judicial legitimacy:** even an accurate recommendation can be unacceptable if parties cannot understand, contest or attribute it.
* **Survey inflation:** “using,” “exploring,” licensed and routinely deployed are not equivalent.

